 "lq": [
  {
   "q": "Find the inverse Z-transform of the following by long division (power series) method. Also, specify the ROC and the nature of the sequence (right-sided/left-sided). a) \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.5z^{-1}}\\) , ROC: \\(|z| > 0.5\\) b) \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1 - 2z}\\) , ROC: \\(|z| < 0.5\\) c) \\(X(z) = \\frac{z}{z^2 - 3z + 2}\\) , ROC: \\(|z| > 2\\)",
   "answer": [
    "a) \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.5z^{-1}} = \\frac{z}{z-0.5}\\) , ROC: |z|>0.5 (right-sided). Perform long division for |z|>0.5 (i.e., in powers of \\(z^{-1}\\) ): Write \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.5z^{-1}}\\) . \\(1 \\div (1 - 0.5z^{-1})\\) : Quotient: \\(1 + 0.5z^{-1} + 0.25z^{-2} + 0.125z^{-3} + ... = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} (0.5)^n z^{-n}\\) . So, \\(x(n) = (0.5)^n u(n)\\) .",
    "b) \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1-2z} = -\\frac{1}{2z} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - (1/(2z))}\\) , ROC: |z|<0.5 implies |1/(2z)|>1? Actually, for ROC |z|<0.5, we expand in positive powers of z (left-sided sequence). Write: \\(X(z) = \\frac{1}{1-2z}\\) . For |2z|<1 i.e., |z|<0.5, we have \\(X(z) = 1 + 2z + 4z^2 + 8z^3 + ... = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} 2^n z^n\\) . But this corresponds to \\(X(z) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{0} x(n) z^{-n}\\) . Let m = -n. Then \\(X(z)=\\sum_{m=0}^{\\infty} x(-m) z^{m}\\) . Comparing: \\(x(-m) = 2^m\\) or \\(x(n) = 2^{-n}\\) for \\(n \\le 0\\) . Equivalently, \\(x(n) = -2^n u(-n-1)\\) (left-sided).",
    "c) \\(X(z) = \\frac{z}{z^2 - 3z + 2} = \\frac{z}{(z-1)(z-2)}\\) , ROC: |z|>2 (right-sided). Perform long division for |z|>2: Divide z by (z^2 - 3z + 2). We want \\(X(z)\\) as a series in \\(z^{-1}\\) . \\(X(z) = z^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - 3z^{-1} + 2z^{-2}}\\) . Divide 1 by (1 - 3z^{-1} + 2z^{-2}): Quotient: \\(1 + 3z^{-1} + 7z^{-2} + 15z^{-3} + 31z^{-4} + ...\\) . So, \\(X(z) = z^{-1}(1 + 3z^{-1} + 7z^{-2} + 15z^{-3} + 31z^{-4} + ...) = z^{-1} + 3z^{-2} + 7z^{-3} + 15z^{-4} + 31z^{-5} + ...\\) . Thus, \\(x(0)=0, x(1)=1, x(2)=3, x(3)=7, x(4)=15, x(5)=31, ...\\) . This corresponds to \\(x(n) = (2^n - 1) u(n-1)\\) ."
   ]
